\begin{abstract}
LLM-based program repair increasingly generates multiple candidate patches, selecting the best via a verifier. Three execution-free alternatives to expensive execution-based verification have emerged: per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), and trajectory-level patch scoring (\traj{}). No prior work compares them under matched conditions.

We present the first systematic comparison across two code LLM families (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder), two complexity levels, and multiple seeds under ${\leq}$8B LoRA fine-tuning. At function level, \cls{} and \cwm{} are statistically equivalent across both families (TOST, 5 seeds): classification is not inherently easier than generation when outputs are short. \traj{} parity is confirmed on Qwen3 after Holm correction (TOST $p{=}0.007$, 5 seeds); the \cls{}--\traj{} gap narrows monotonically from 3.11\pp{} (4B) to 1.07\pp{} (14B), though model-contingent on DeepSeek-Coder (gap 2.69\pp{}). At repository level, a significant interaction ($z{=}-23.7$, DeepSeek-Coder) confirms this equivalence collapses: the \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens to 18--26\pp{} across 2--3 seeds per model under our ${\leq}$8B LoRA protocol, driven by format collapse. At 14B (3 seeds), all three formulations converge (maximum gap 1.07\pp{}), consistent with capacity-dependent resolution of formulation differences.

Error complementarity and a failure mode taxonomy motivate future work on complexity-aware formulation routing, though simple heuristics capture $<$0.4\pp{} of oracle advantage.
\end{abstract}

